Ans1. A closure is a function that remembers the variables of its outer function, even after the outer function has finished running.

function outer() {
  let count = 0;
  function inner() {
    count++;
    console.log(count);
  }
  return inner;
}

const counter = outer();
counter(); // 1
counter(); // 2

Here, inner still remembers count even after outer is finished.

Ans2. A Promise is an object that represents the result of an asynchronous task, which can finish now or later. It has three states:
1. Pending - the task is still running, with no result yet.
2. Fulfilled - the task finished successfully.
3. Rejected - the task failed with an error.

let p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  resolve("Done");
});
p.then(res => console.log(res)).catch(err => console.log(err));

Ans3. async/await is a way to write asynchronous code that looks like normal code. An async function always returns a Promise, and await pauses the function until the Promise gives its result.

It makes working with Promises easier because:
- We do not need to write long .then() chains.
- The code is easier to read and understand.
- Errors can be handled with simple try...catch.

async function getData() {
  try {
    let res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/data");
    let data = await res.json();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error);
  }
}

Ans4. The Global Execution Context is the first and default environment created when JavaScript code starts running. All code that is not inside any function runs here. It does two things:
1. Creates the global object (window in the browser).
2. Creates the this keyword, which points to the global object.

There is only one Global Execution Context in a program. It is created in two phases: the memory creation phase and the code execution phase.

Ans5. Currying is a technique in which a function with many arguments is changed into a series of functions that each take one argument.

// Normal function
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

// Curried function
function addCurry(a) {
  return function (b) {
    return a + b;
  };
}

console.log(addCurry(2)(3)); // 5

Ans6. jQuery is a small and fast JavaScript library. It makes tasks like DOM selection, event handling, animations and AJAX much easier, with less code ("write less, do more").

Its significance in web development:
- It makes JavaScript code short and simple.
- It works the same in all browsers (cross-browser support).
- It makes DOM changes, effects and animations easy.
- It makes AJAX calls simple.

$("#btn").click(function () {
  $("p").hide();
});

Ans7. A component in React is a small, reusable piece of the user interface. A web page is made by joining many components together. Its types are:
1. Functional Component - a simple JavaScript function that returns JSX. It uses hooks like useState to manage state.
2. Class Component - a class that extends React.Component and uses a render() method to return JSX.

function Hello() {
  return <h1>Hello World</h1>;
}

Ans8. The Virtual DOM is a light copy of the real DOM that is kept in memory by React.

How it improves performance:
1. When something changes, React first updates the Virtual DOM.
2. It compares the new Virtual DOM with the old one (this is called diffing).
3. It finds only the parts that changed.
4. It updates only those parts in the real DOM.

Updating the real DOM is slow, so updating only the changed parts makes the application fast.

Ans9. JSX stands for JavaScript XML. It lets us write HTML-like code inside JavaScript.

const element = <h1>Hello</h1>;

Browsers cannot understand JSX directly because it is not valid JavaScript. Browsers only understand normal JavaScript. So, JSX must first be converted into normal JavaScript using a tool like Babel.

Ans10. Props (short for properties) are the way to pass data from a parent component to a child component. They are written like HTML attributes.

function Welcome(props) {
  return <h1>Hello {props.name}</h1>;
}

<Welcome name="Ritik" />

